browser_download_url":"https://github.com/mxym/math/releases/download/collective-unitary-negativity-rate-preprint-v1/PREPRINT_BINDING.json"},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mxym/math/releases/assets/627114920","id":627114920,"node_id":"RA_kwDOU-09Qs4lYQOo","name":"SHA256SUMS","label":"","uploader":{"login":"mxym","id":77926411,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc3OTI2NDEx","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/77926411?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym","html_url":"https://github.com/mxym","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/mxym/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"content_type":"text/plain","state":"uploaded","size":332,"digest":"sha256:3dfd9df9f1dabd02ae299d5ad4ca985ab20b828fdc34d3056db801a34807df8b","download_count":0,"created_at":"2026-10-10T05:39:57Z","updated_at":"2026-10-10T05:39:57Z","browser_download_url":"https://github.com/mxym/math/releases/download/collective-unitary-negativity-rate-preprint-v1/SHA256SUMS"}],"tarball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mxym/math/tarball/collective-unitary-negativity-rate-preprint-v1","zipball_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mxym/math/zipball/collective-unitary-negativity-rate-preprint-v1","body":"Eight-page analytic preprint by Yongxian Zhang: **The collective-unitary logarithmic-negativity rate of every bipartite spectrum**.\n\nFor every density matrix rho on C^m tensor C^n, with 2 <= m <= n and spectrum p (zero eigenvalues allowed), the paper proves\n\n```text\nlim_{k -> infinity} (1/k) max_U log ||(U rho^{tensor k} U*)^{Gamma_{A^k:B^k}}||_1\n= min_{1 <= alpha <= 2} [log m + (alpha-1) log n + log sum_i p_i^alpha] / alpha.\n```\n\nFor m=n=d this becomes `(1/2) log(d^2 Tr(rho^2))`. A fixed blockwise Bell output basis suffices at the exponential scale; only the assignment of eigenvalues changes. The unequal-dimension formula can require an interior Renyi parameter, and an exact 2 x 8 example has alpha=3/2.\n\nThe lower bound is supported by a dimension-uniform one-shot spectral-prefix estimate, using random Bell-basis projections and the published matrix Bernstein theorem of Tropp. Spectral types match the Schatten-norm upper bounds. This is an analytic proof, **not a Lean-formalized theorem or external peer review**. Exact ancillary calculations and deliberate corrupted-source controls passed independent CI run 38027937313. Those checks support the calculations and are not a certificate of the full unbounded proof.\n\nPublication commit: `3fac265d142cbc5ce48700abce5cced59a1a4b8f`.\nAncillary-tested source: `a09f51daa1deed207ad39a9360ab8efd87d496f2`.\nDirectory: `research/collective-unitary-negativity-rate/`.\nCI: https://github.com/mxym/math/actions/runs/38027937313\n\nThe source archive contains the complete proof, standalone LaTeX, exact standard-library checker, source-mutation tests, source hashes, and literal CI records. From its extracted root:\n\n```sh\npython3 verify_sources.py\npython3 check.py\npython3 -O test_checker.py\n```\n\nCompile `main.tex` twice with pdfLaTeX, or rebuild from `PROOF.md` using `python3 build_manuscript.py` with Pandoc and pdfLaTeX. `PREPRINT_BINDING.json` and `SHA256SUMS` bind the PDF and source to the publication and tested commits.\n\nThe operation class permits unrestricted global unitaries but no extra ancilla, measurement, conditioning, or discarded system. The theorem does **not** claim a local distillation rate, efficient circuits, the exact one-copy maximum, APPT=AS, or a solution of the finite-copy higher-dimensional APPT purity conjecture. Prior finite-copy activation and finite-spectrum entanglement bounds are explicitly credited. No exhaustive novelty/priority certification, new DOI, or arXiv submission is asserted. Author affiliation, independent funding status, and AI-assistance disclosure are included.\n\nThe earlier complete qutrit Lean Release and its public preprint DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23269470 remain unchanged and are separate results.\n"}